  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   The challenge is to minimize Erd\u0151s's minimum overlap problem via step function design. We're provided a binary step function parent (42 samples) and need to lower its overlap constant (C=0.38085). Our core idea is to use a binary tournament-inspired optimization with survival-of-the-fittest selection of regions where overlap decreases. We start with the parent's grid but extend the parameter space to non-binary values via interpolation, then apply continuous optimization with adaptive shifting. The main bottleneck is ensuring neighborhoods offer lower overlap while maintaining sum constraint.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   - Variables: h \\in [0,1]^{n\\_points} e.g., h[i] values at grid points\n   - Constraint: sum(h) = n\\_points/2 (fixed integral = 1)\n   - Objective: Maximize overlap for adjacent sequences (minimize worst-case overlap)\n   - Derivatives: Local gradient computation from two-point breathing operations\n   - Solution space: n\\_points-dimensional continuous space with binary-like interpolation\n   - Solver: Limited-memory BFGS with box constraints (scipy.optimize.minimize)\n   - Repair: Sum constraint enforced by projection\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   a. Input handling: fetch initial_h_values if provided, default n_points=42\n   b. Pre-scale parent to satisfy integral constraint automatically\n   c. Build a pool of candidate neighborhoods via:\n        - Neighborhood extraction: windowed slice of initial parent with padding\n        - Sensor-tuning: perturb parent via interpolation\n        - Grid morphing: linear interpolation between random grid points\n   d. Evaluation function:\n        C5 = max_k np.dot(h, 1-h) (full convolution correlate)\n        * Multiply by dx=2.0/42 for integral scaling\n   e. Batch optimization:\n        - SCSR algorithm loop:\n          1. Start with initial parent\n          2. Generate neighboring grid by shifting selection window\n          3. Evaluate overlap at center and margin\n          4. Select those with reduced margin overlap\n          5. Morph the best candidate via iterative breathing\n   f. Arestraint: maintain valid solution via re-projection\n   g. Timer: budget_s limits optimization cycles\n\n4. Search Choices & Variations\n   - Neighborhood size: 20% of n_points (default)\n   - Morph factor: temperature-parameterized search range (0.1-1.0)\n   - Neighborhood evaluation: check \u00b150% shift range\n   - Morph intensity: adaptive based on weight of best overlap\n   - Random grid interpolation: random nearest-neighbor searches\n   - Output variation: compare binary vs continuous interpolation\n\n5. Compute Budget & Robustness\n   - Time complexity: O(n_points^2) per evaluation\n   - Schedule: Start with parent's worst-case overlap and cycle until time exhausted\n   - Kill condition: terminal improvement < 0.0001 in 1000 cycles (10-minute mark)\n   - Fallback: return initial parent if optimization fails\n   - Projection: solve constrained least-squares for box and sum\n   - Back-up evaluation: Manhattan-based least increase\n\n6. Validation & Output\n   Validate sum(h) == 21 (42 samples) via exact projection\n   Compute C5 full convolution (S=h, T=1-h)\n   Return:\n     h_values (numpy float array of length n_points)\n     c5_bound (scaled max overlap)\n     n_points\n   Verify: h[i] \\in [0,1] \\forall i",
